course: dictionary
lesson: 12635
title: Words that put people down
level: primary-6
stars: 2
explain: Disparagement is a noun. It means saying that someone or something is not good or not important, in an unkind or scornful way. It is another way of saying you are putting someone down. Read the words around it to find the clues.
explain: «Her constant disparagement of his ideas made Sam stop sharing them.» The clues are made Sam stop sharing and constant. Sam stopped sharing because the ideas were being put down again and again, so disparagement must mean unkind put-downs. Check it: swap in unkind put-downs, and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: A disparagement is also one single remark like that. «Calling the drawing worthless was a disparagement.» The opposite of disparagement is praise, which is saying that someone or something is good. Disparagement is a formal word, so you will meet it more often in writing than in talk.
fact: Disparagement means saying that someone or something is not good or not important, in an unkind way.
fact: Disparagement is a noun. A disparagement is one unkind remark of this kind.
fact: The opposite of disparagement is praise.
fact: In stories, constant disparagement of others often shows that a character is mean or proud.
fact: Words of disparagement can hurt, so authors use them to show how a character treats other people.
exercise: «Her constant disparagement of his ideas made Sam stop sharing them.» What does disparagement mean? | *Unkind talk that puts his ideas down | Kind praise of his ideas | A long speech about the weather | Helping Sam with his homework
exercise: Which remark is a disparagement? | «Well done for finishing your work.» | «What a lovely day it is.» | *«Your painting is worthless.» | «You are so kind to help me.»
exercise: «The proud king made constant disparagement of his workers.» What does this show about the king? | He is very shy | *He is mean and proud | He is very generous | He loves music
test: «The critic's disparagement of the play was unfair, because it had many good scenes.» What is the best meaning of disparagement here? | *An unkind put-down | A sleepy yawn | Huge praise | A new scene in the play
test: What is the opposite of disparagement? | Hurry | Laughter | Confusion | *Praise
exam: Which word best describes a remark that makes someone feel small and worthless? | *Disparagement | Gratitude | Celebration | Invitation
